---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'

const boards = assertion('twelve-boards-and-nine-ballots')
---

<Article slug="twelve-boards-nine-ballots">
  <AssertionCard assertion={boards} />
  <p>
    Twelve school districts hold ground in Allen County. Nine are centred here and three are not:
    Columbus Grove and Pandora-Gilboa are Putnam County districts that reach across the line, and
    Waynesfield-Goshen is an Auglaize one. Together they cover 489 people in Monroe Township, 39
    in Richland, and a corner of Auglaize Township.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site could not name those three boards, said so on each district's page, and named the
    neighbouring county's roster as the source that would. It was wrong. The Ohio boards of
    elections lookup answers two ways — precinct by precinct, or by district type — and Allen
    County's own roster carries all twelve boards under the second. Read across all eighty-eight
    precincts it carries nine, and never those three, because no elector in this county votes on
    them.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the roster answers two different questions and this site had asked only one. What an Allen
    County voter fills is 218 seats. What governs the districts holding ground here is fifteen
    seats more. Both numbers are right and they are not interchangeable.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more thing came out of asking two counties the same question. The fifteen people appear on
    the Allen roster and on the Putnam and Auglaize rosters, and eight of the fifteen are spelled
    identically. Angie Basinger in Lima is Angela Lynn Basinger in Ottawa; Kevin Ewing is Kevin D
    Ewing; Michelle Steinke is Michelle R Steinke. Nothing in the source carries an identifier for
    a person, so matching one across two counties is a guess about a middle initial.
  </p>
</Article>
